Samuel Smith: Because you have waiting periods for the spec to move forward, and if you… so… so those waiting periods then start to…

485
01:01:01.050 --> 01:01:08.339
Samuel Smith: You know, do things like, you know, delay you from getting the spec to a certain point, and maybe that doesn't matter.

486
01:01:08.770 --> 01:01:15.190
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): What if we, released the spec only with a thing that said.

487
01:01:15.440 --> 01:01:19.770
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): For worked examples, go see the following, location.

488
01:01:19.990 --> 01:01:23.860
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): And then the worked examples were moved out of the spec entirely.

489
01:01:24.630 --> 01:01:28.119
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): Is that, too radical of a move?

490
01:01:30.660 --> 01:01:35.250
Samuel Smith: No, I don't… I don't think so. They're formative, so they don't have to be in the spec.

491
01:01:36.250 --> 01:01:38.320
Kent Bull: I like that, just… go ahead.

492
01:01:38.320 --> 01:01:53.220
Samuel Smith: People don't like it in general, because if they download the spec, then they have to go to some other repo to go look them up, but I don't think that's a… but I think people who don't like that aren't, you know, that's not a requirement, that's just some people don't like it.

493
01:01:53.400 --> 01:02:04.699
Samuel Smith: But if you… if you really do a good job, what tends to happen, and the reason they don't like it, is it tends to happen that when people decide to move the informative examples outside the spec.

494
01:02:04.990 --> 01:02:21.979
Samuel Smith: they tend not to get supported, because if they're in the spec, then lots of… lots of eyes look at them, and people go, this doesn't work, that doesn't work, right? And so it gets… it gets attention that it wouldn't get when it's outside the spec, because what… what often happens, you move it outside the spec, and then nobody works on it.

495
01:02:22.650 --> 01:02:25.080
Samuel Smith: And then it's sitting there, you know.

496
01:02:25.270 --> 01:02:37.810
Samuel Smith: 6 months later, and people are reading the spec, and they go, well, where are your work examples? And everybody says, oh, well, we moved them here, but we never got around to finishing them, or supporting them, or making them work well.

497
01:02:37.870 --> 01:02:56.130
Samuel Smith: And so it's more a laziness enforcement mechanism to put them in the spec, because the spec can't get released unless you do it, so it makes a hard requirement to do it. So if you have the discipline that says you're going to do the worked examples, and you have the resources to make sure they happen, then nobody's going to complain.

498
01:02:56.330 --> 01:02:59.670
Samuel Smith: If the worked examples are done, you know.

499
01:03:00.440 --> 01:03:02.390
Samuel Smith: At the same time that the spec is.

500
01:03:03.600 --> 01:03:12.279
Kent Bull: I like the idea of keeping them in the spec, because some… a great engineer told me a number of years ago that that which is not automatically enforced, tends to degrade.

501
01:03:12.820 --> 01:03:23.930
Kent Bull: I like… and to me, it's… we've got a full example section here. We could say there's version 1, and then have a version 2, just have it be in the spec. I like the idea of separating it too, Daniel, but if I had to think of the trade-offs.

502
01:03:24.780 --> 01:03:27.679
Kent Bull: It seems safer to keep it in.

503
01:03:28.130 --> 01:03:31.260
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): That's fine, I wasn't really advocating, I was just speculating.

504
01:03:32.950 --> 01:03:39.580
Kent Bull: It would make the specs shorter and easier to review if they were taken out, but keeping them in does force us to have more discipline.

505
01:03:42.270 --> 01:03:59.340
Kent Bull: So what I'll do then is I'll just start it as a version 2 part in this spec here. We'll have a full example for version 1 and a full example for version 2. Even if some of the version 2 stuff hasn't fully settled, which I think it already has, but I'll just put in there what we currently have, and then we can review that and see what we think.

506
01:04:01.710 --> 01:04:09.409
Kent Bull: Is there anything else we should add before we close? I know it's 4 minutes over already, and there's a Cary Foundation setup call after this.

507
01:04:10.020 --> 01:04:13.360
Kent Bull: If there's nothing else, just last call.

508
01:04:14.400 --> 01:04:20.780
Kent Bull: I'll add one more action item, I'll let everybody go, and then I'll write an action item here for me to respond to this

509
01:04:21.100 --> 01:04:29.289
Kent Bull: So I already made a comment here, Daniel, I'll put a response here, and I will put an action item for me to add the version 2 worked example.

510
01:04:29.890 --> 01:04:32.410
Kent Bull: Great. That's all from me. Anything from anybody else?

511
01:04:34.880 --> 01:04:35.810
Kent Bull: Alright.

512
01:04:36.090 --> 01:04:37.190
Kent Bull: Have a good day.

